Z||000010||Wannabe||10-26-2000||09:20 PM||||Yes! Thanks for the pic, it is very helpful. WT...looking forward to the update on the install. Thanks all!||||reg||1
Z||000011||kmunson56||10-31-2000||12:39 AM||||Wannabe,<BR>I posted a question a little while ago about the same thing. I have been checking further back in the Forum and found a thread from Will where he put a detailed procedure on how to install the 88 front suspension. I plan to put both the front and rear suspension from an 88 into my 87 GT. William T. Thorn,III has been a great help. I am doing it a little differently since I work at a dealership and have welding equipment available. If you have the complete cradle with the struts out of an 88 it can be done with minor modifications. The front suspension takes a little more but it can be done.<P>Kris A. Munson, Sr.||||reg||1
Z||000012||bHooper||10-31-2000||06:50 AM||||Thanks for the pic!  That helpsed me to understand the geometry... very different from what I imagined.<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000013||Wannabe||10-31-2000||02:44 PM||||Actually, I had in mind to machine adapters that will bolt to the '84-87 rear knuckles and allow the use of two lateral and one trailing link, just like the '88 suspension...kind of a variation on Ryane's defunct bump steer kit, but with the individual links rather than a triangulated control arm. This set up could be bolted into any Fiero, using the without the need to swap in an '88 subframe and adapt the struts. You would not get the improved camber curve if the '88, but I also have a simple solution for improving that.||||reg||1
